content is stored in blobs, repositories in trees
"git preserva história estruturada e com autoria, mensagens e relações entre estados"
!! git identifies objects using SHA-1 hashes, with 40 characters

object | contains | related codes |
-----
blob | contents of a file | ´git hash-object´ ´git cat-file-p´
tree | directory structure: names, permissions and hashes | ´git cat-file -p <tree hash>´
commit | history. points to a tree and it's parent commit | ´git log´ ´git cat-file -p <hash>´
tag | named reference to an important commit | ´git tag´

the .git (the repository itself) contains two dimensions: the visible workspace and the hidden versioning infrastructure
it is composed of these sections:
    - ´objects/´: database containing the files, the blobs, the trees, etc
    - ´refs/´: references to trees and commits
    - ´HEAD´: information of the current 
    - ´config´: local repository configurations
    - ´index´: for the staging area

O histórico git é um grafo acíclico direcionado (DAG)

A file can be staged and modified at the same time, c'est-à-dire, staging a file creates a file as a snapshot
This allows for atomic and selected commits.

there are 4 basic states the files and folders can be in:
    - untracked: git is not tracking this guy, mostly bc of the repository's .gitignore file
    - modified
    - staged
    - committed

